**Rows to canonical vector**: I read `yin.vm.ast-walker/vm-load-rows` as instructed; it converts a *different* row shape (`{:root id :rows {id row}}` semantic-bytecode rows) back into an AST tree, not into a canonical instruction vector — it is not the precedent this step needs, and I say so in the namespace docstring. The actual named-path precedent for turning `lower`'s `:yin.code/*` datom rows into the canonical positional vector is `yin.vm.semantic/load-image`'s private local `canonical` closure (index the batch by entity, sort by `:yin.code/pc`, read each entity's operands off `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` with `:pc`-kind operands resolved to positions). That helper is private in a namespace this phase may only read, so I reimplemented the equivalent step as `named-canonical-vector` (public, so tests can reuse it independently).

**Scope reconstruction**: `closure-body-ranges` and `layout-conforms?` reproduce `yin.vm.completion`'s private `closure-ranges`/`layout-conforms?` algorithm exactly, generalized to operate on either a named vector (`:closure` params at index 1) or a de Bruijn image (`:closure` arity at index 1) since both carry the body pc at index 2. `body-owner` derives pc → immediate enclosing `:closure` pc from those ranges (the `segment-scope` precedent, without the address/relation wrapping since no Datalog is needed here). `chain-of` walks `body-owner` from a pc outward to build the innermost-first enclosing-closure-pc chain — the explicit outermost→innermost reversal section 3 requires, documented at its definition. `resolve-var` builds the innermost-first parameter-vector stack from that chain and calls `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name`, the one reused projection helper.

**Side table**: `{pc {:kind :closure, :params [sym...], :source src}}` for every `:closure` pc and `{pc {:kind :var, :source src}}` for every `:var` pc. No name is stored per `:var` occurrence — `lift` derives it from the owning `:closure`'s recorded (or synthesized) parameter vector at the resolved position, which `resolve-name`'s own contract guarantees is the occurrence's exact original spelling. This avoids a second, redundant name record.

**Lift**: `lift-params` computes pc → parameter vector for every `:closure`, using the side table's entry when present and arity-matching, else synthesizing fresh, globally-unique names (`g__<pc>_<i>`) fresh against the image's free-name set and every other synthesized parameter. `lift-once` then rewrites `:load-bound`/`:load-free` back to `:var` and `:closure`'s arity back to its parameter vector; everything else copies unchanged. Public `lift` tries the supplied side table first, verifies it round-trips (`rewrite` of the result reproduces the exact same de Bruijn image), and falls back to full synthesis — which always round-trips by construction — when it does not.

**Correctness law, per program** (`worked-example`, `duplicate-param` = `(fn [x x] x)`, `free-variable`, `nested-closure`):
- Own side table: `lift-with-the-adapters-own-side-table-is-exact` asserts `= nv lifted` (exact equality), proven mathematically because `resolve-name`'s `position-in` only ever returns a position whose stored symbol equals the occurrence's own name, so reading it back is always exact — verified for the duplicate-parameter case too.
- No side table: `lift-with-synthesized-names-is-alpha-equivalent` asserts the synthesized result differs in spelling but `(= image (:debruijn (rewrite lifted)))` — the operational alpha-equivalence definition this bytecode form admits (identical binding structure re-derived).
- `a-foreign-side-table-that-fails-to-round-trip-falls-back-to-synthesis` checks a corrupted side table is discarded rather than trusted.

